{#
    The top bar's right-hand side: search, the one prominent action, and who is
    signed in.

    The design's Manage Balance pill is not here, for the reason Phase 8 gave —
    Renovo tracks what is due, not a balance, so the pill would be a button for
    a number that does not exist. The slot goes to quick-add, which is the
    action a bar over a subscription tracker should actually offer.

    Quick-add is an anchor, not a button, and that is deliberate: with the
    script it opens the dialog that loads the real subscription form, and
    without it the same click goes to the same form as a page. The one
    definition of what a subscription needs is reached either way.

    The search form is its own form and submits to the subscriptions list, which
    is the collection there is anything to search. On the list itself the page's
    own filter is the finer instrument, and `/` prefers it — see
    `public/assets/app.js`.
#}
<div class="topbar-actions">
    {% if can(scope, 'subscription.view') %}
        <form class="topbar-search" method="get" action="/subscriptions" role="search">
            <span class="topbar-search-icon" data-icon="search" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <input type="search" name="q" aria-label="{{ t('nav.search') }}"
                   placeholder="{{ t('nav.search') }}">
        </form>
    {% endif %}

    {% if can(scope, 'subscription.create') %}
        <a class="button topbar-add" href="/subscriptions/new"
           data-opens-dialog="quick-add">
            <span class="nav-icon" data-icon="add" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="topbar-add-label">{{ t('subscriptions.add_subscription') }}</span>
        </a>
    {% endif %}

    <div class="topbar-account">
        <span class="topbar-name">{{ current_user.displayName }}</span>
        <form method="post" action="/logout" class="inline">
            {{ csrf_field() }}
            <button type="submit" class="button button-quiet">{{ t('nav.sign_out') }}</button>
        </form>
    </div>
</div>
